Firmware-update and cloud-service redirection
=============================================

/etc/nsswitch.conf is "hosts: files nisplus nis dns", so a name resolved in
/etc/hosts wins over DNS. That makes /etc/hosts the switch for pointing the
panel at your own server, with no binary patching.

Do not put prose in /etc/hosts. /etc/rc.d/init.d/network rewrites it at boot:

    51:  sed -e 's,.*hostname,'$IPADDR0'     '`hostname`','
    58:  sed -e 's,.*gateway0,'$GATEWAY0'     gateway0,'
    82,106,125,144: the same for gateway1..gateway4

The patterns are unanchored, so ANY line containing "hostname" or "gateway0"
through "gateway4" is rewritten, comments included. The v8 image shipped a
comment containing the word "hostname" and got back the live entry

    203.0.113.5     6285. It asks an

6285 is the value of HOSTNAME exported by /etc/rc.d/rc.conf; the hostname
service runs before network, so that is what the substitution used.

Which names to override
-----------------------

The panel holds no literal firmware-download name. It asks an AlarmNet "AUI
redirector" where to go, then fetches over plain HTTP with GET and Range
requests. The redirectors are the names to override:

    auiredir1.alarmnet.com
    auiredir2.alarmnet.com
    auiredir3.alarmnet.com
    auiredirtest.alarmnet.com

The same three names, paired with their addresses, are also in
/srv_info.conf, which the firmware parses itself (ReadServInfo). Editing that
file is the other route, and is the better one if you want the panel to reach
a different address for the SAME name.

Other vendor services, listed so they are not a mystery later:

    gw.mylanconnect.com          remote-access IP update, /php/VAM/*.php
    tccaps.honeywell.com         Total Connect
    ie3bvutux003.honeywell.com
    www.tuxedotouchtoolkit.com

Redirecting a name to a host that does not answer makes the panel retry. The
update client treats DNS failure as non-fatal and starts a two-hour retry
timer ("DNS error 2 hour query timer start"), so a wrong entry degrades
updates rather than breaking the panel.

/etc/hosts lives in the root filesystem, so it survives reboots but is
replaced by the next app2 reflash. Keep a copy.
